     1	VALIDATED
     2	
     3	## What is proven
     4	
     5	Under the supplied MPY semantics, `get_positive` is partially correct for every
     6	arbitrary finite list whose elements are MPY `Int` or `Float` values. It returns
     7	a fresh list containing exactly the input elements whose MPY comparison with
     8	`0.0` is true, in their original order and with duplicates preserved.
     9	
    10	This is an unbounded symbolic theorem over `VS:ValSeq`; it is not a collection
    11	of fixed-length claims. The exact translated function definition, lookup,
    12	argument binding, allocation, loop, `append`, return, frame pop, heap result,
    13	exception cell, and exit code all execute in the target claim.
    14	
    15	This is a partial-correctness result. Termination is not a conclusion of the K
    16	reachability claims.
    17	
    18	## Formal claim and scope
    19	
    20	The required entry claim is `SPEC.get-positive` in `spec.k`:
    21	
    22	```k
    23	<k>
    24	  FuncDef("get_positive", Params("l"), EXACT-BODY)
    25	  ~> Call(Name("get_positive"), list(VS:ValSeq))
    26	  => ref(0)
    27	</k>
    28	<heap> .Map => 0 |-> list(filterPositive(.ValSeq, VS)) </heap>
    29	requires numericVals(VS)
    30	```
    31	
    32	The other cells state that the call starts in the module environment with the
    33	supplied builtins, restores the caller environment and empty stack, leaves
    34	`NoExc`, and leaves exit code `0`.
    35	
    36	Validation scope:
    37	
    38	- Program boundary: the exact `FuncDef` and real call through MPY, including
    39	  every operation defined in `solution.mpy`.
    40	- Input domain: all finite `ValSeq` values satisfying `numericVals`, i.e. every
    41	  element is an MPY `Int` or `Float`. Length is symbolic and unbounded.
    42	- Observable final state: returned reference, referenced list contents, heap
    43	  allocation counter, caller environment, stack, return state, exception, and
    44	  exit code.
    45	- Intended property: stable filtering by strict positivity, preserving values,
    46	  order, and multiplicity.
    47	
    48	`SPEC.filter-loop` is the circular loop invariant. Starting with accumulator
    49	contents `ACC` and remaining input `VS`, it ends with accumulator contents
    50	`filterPositive(ACC, VS)`. Its frame pins the exact three local bindings
    51	`l`, `positive`, and `x`, and the exact loop body.
    52	
    53	## Proof-extension inventory
    54	
    55	### `numericVal` and `numericVals`
    56	
    57	- Class: definitional summaries.
    58	- Semantic role: describe the input domain; they replace no execution.
    59	- Domain: all `Val` and `ValSeq` terms.
    60	- Matched context and justification scope: pure function terms only; no
    61	  continuation, stack, binding, or cell context.
    62	- Equations: `numericVal(V)` is `isInt(V) orBool isFloat(V)`;
    63	  `numericVals` is the usual empty/cons recursion.
    64	- State footprint: none.
    65	- Value influence: the entry precondition, loop invariant precondition,
    66	  `positiveNumeric` default guard, and dynamic dispatch guard.
    67	- Value justification: built-in sort predicates and exhaustive, disjoint
    68	  empty/cons equations.
    69	- Dependents: every target and connection claim.
    70	- Validation: recursive descent is strict; constructor cases cover all
    71	  `ValSeq`; concrete Int/Float cases occur in `smoke.py` and the differential
    72	  corpus.
    73	
    74	### `positiveNumeric`
    75	
    76	- Class: definitional summary.
    77	- Semantic role: names the Boolean positivity atom; it replaces no program
    78	  operation by itself.
    79	- Domain: all `Val` values.
    80	- Matched context and justification scope: pure function terms only.
    81	- Equations: Int maps to `gtF(intToF(I), 0.0)`, Float maps to
    82	  `gtF(F, 0.0)`, and nonnumeric values map to `false`.
    83	- Coverage and overlap: Int and Float are disjoint; the third guard is their
    84	  exact negation, so the total equations are exhaustive and nonoverlapping.
    85	- State footprint: none.
    86	- Value influence: selects `append` versus skip and therefore affects the
    87	  returned list.
    88	- Value justification: on the formal numeric domain, the bridge-free
    89	  `compare-*` and `applycmp-*` connection claims establish equality to fixed
    90	  MPY execution. The underlying `intToF` and `gtF` meanings remain the supplied
    91	  external float trust boundary.
    92	- Dependents: `filterPositive`, the dispatch twin, both target claims, and the
    93	  negative probes.
    94	- Validation: all connection claims print `#Top`; LLVM/CPython differential
    95	  evidence has zero mismatches.
    96	
    97	### `filterPositive`
    98	
    99	- Class: definitional summary.
   100	- Semantic role: describes the final accumulator; it does not replace the
   101	  loop or `append`.
   102	- Domain: all accumulator and remaining `ValSeq` pairs.
   103	- Matched context and justification scope: pure function terms only.
   104	- Equations: empty input returns the accumulator; a positive head is appended
   105	  with the fixed `valSeqConcat`; a nonpositive head is skipped.
   106	- Coverage and overlap: empty and cons are disjoint; `positiveNumeric(V)` and
   107	  its negation are exhaustive and disjoint. Every recursive equation consumes
   108	  one cons cell.
   109	- State footprint: none.
   110	- Value influence: fixes the target heap contents.
   111	- Value justification: its equations are the mathematical stable-filter
   112	  definition and use the same connected positivity predicate as execution.
   113	- Dependents: the loop invariant and entry claim.
   114	- Validation: the loop circularity prints `#Top`; false-result and body
   115	  mutations are rejected.
   116	
   117	### Guarded `applyCmp` dispatch twin
   118	
   119	Exact rule:
   120	
   121	```k
   122	rule applyCmp(">", V:Val, 0.0) => positiveNumeric(V)
   123	  requires numericVal(V)
   124	  [simplification]
   125	```
   126	
   127	- Class: operational bridge, classified conservatively because it accelerates
   128	  a fixed semantic function during symbolic execution.
   129	- Domain: exactly `applyCmp(">", V, 0.0)` with `V` an Int or Float.
   130	- Matched context: a pure function occurrence in any congruent term. It reads
   131	  no cells, changes no continuation, introduces no control effect, and has no
   132	  binding assumptions.
   133	- Justification scope: the union of universal Int and Float connection claims
   134	  in `spec-connection.k`, compiled with main module `VERIFICATION-BASE`.
   135	  That module does not import this twin.
   136	- Context containment: the connection claims establish the pure `applyCmp`
   137	  equation for both static sorts; pure equality is congruent in every matched
   138	  context. End-to-end `Compare` claims also cover evaluation and an arbitrary
   139	  continuation.
   140	- State footprint: none read, written, preserved, or abstracted.
   141	- Value influence: controls the branch and final list.
   142	- Value justification: bridge-free Int and Float `applyCmp` and `Compare`
   143	  claims collectively cover the complete `numericVal` guard.
   144	- Dependents: loop invariant and entry claim.
   145	- Control validation: end-to-end connection claims preserve arbitrary trailing
   146	  computation and every framed configuration cell.
   147	- Value validation: the false-result probe rejects the opposite result for a
   148	  satisfiable `positiveNumeric(I)` witness; LLVM evidence includes positive,
   149	  zero, and negative Int/Float values.
   150	
   151	### `SPEC.filter-loop`
   152	
   153	- Class: derived reachability lemma used coinductively as the loop circularity.
   154	- Domain: arbitrary numeric `VS`, arbitrary accumulator `ACC`, and the exact
   155	  reachable plain function frame and heap binding.
   156	- Matched context: exact `#loop`, target, body, local scope shape, and
   157	  accumulator heap object; arbitrary trailing continuation and framed
   158	  unrelated cells are preserved.
   159	- Justification scope and context containment: the claim itself quantifies over
   160	  the arbitrary continuation and framed cells. `kprove` checks its empty and
   161	  cons cases under fixed loop, binding, comparison, method-call, and heap
   162	  rules.
   163	- State footprint: reads `x` and `positive`; updates `x` and the list at `H`;
   164	  preserves `l`, the positive reference, environment, unrelated heap entries,
   165	  allocation counter, stack, return state, exception, exit code, and
   166	  continuation.
   167	- Value influence: fixes the accumulator and final returned list.
   168	- Dependents: `SPEC.get-positive`.
   169	- Validation: focused proof prints `#Top`; removing `append` makes the
   170	  corresponding result claim fail.
   171	
   172	No rule intercepts `FuncDef`, `Call`, `For`, `#loop`, `append`, `Return`, or
   173	frame popping.
